      <h3>Frequently Asked Questions</h3>
      <div class="faq-list">
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>Can blood sugar drops cause 3 AM awakenings?</summary>
          <p>Yes. Nocturnal hypoglycemia (a drop in blood glucose during the night) triggers the adrenal glands to release adrenaline and cortisol to mobilize glucose from the liver. Eating a small handful of raw almonds or pumpkin seeds before bed can help stabilize blood sugar.</p>
        </details>
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>Why does my heart beat so hard when I wake up at night?</summary>
          <p>Adrenaline increases myocardial contractility (the force of heart muscle contraction). Even if your heart rate is only moderately elevated, the beats feel intense and loud against your ribs in the quiet bedroom.</p>
        </details>
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>Should I check my phone when I wake up panicked?</summary>
          <p>Avoid checking email, social media, or news feeds, as they inject fresh emotional stimuli into an already vulnerable brain. If you must use a screen, keep brightness at minimum and use single-purpose grounding tools like our <a href="/calm/">Circuit Breaker</a>.</p>
        </details>
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>What is sleep maintenance insomnia?</summary>
          <p>Sleep maintenance insomnia refers to difficulty staying asleep throughout the night, characterized by waking up after only a few hours and struggling to fall back asleep. It is frequently linked to elevated cortisol and fragmented sleep architecture.</p>
        </details>
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>How can I prevent waking up at 3 AM?</summary>
          <p>Reduce late-afternoon caffeine, avoid alcohol within 4 hours of bedtime, maintain a consistent wake-up schedule, and practice an evening cognitive offload with our <a href="/journal/">Brain Dump Tool</a>.</p>
        </details>
      </div>
    </div>
